1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,840
PART 2

2
00:00:02,840 --> 00:00:04,772
Woman:

3
00:00:04,772 --> 00:00:09,500
I'm very pleased to welcome this evening's guest speaker, Mark Logan,

4
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:13,950
who's going to tell us about the recent transformation of Minster Park.

5
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:15,610
Over to you, Mark.

6
00:00:15,610 --> 00:00:15,953
Mark:

7
00:00:15,953 --> 00:00:16,416
Thank you.

8
00:00:16,416 --> 00:00:20,950
I'm sure you're all familiar with Minster Park.

9
00:00:20,950 --> 00:00:24,490
It's been a feature of the city for well over a century,

10
00:00:24,490 --> 00:00:29,890
and has been the responsibility of the city council for most of that time.

11
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:35,430
What perhaps isn't so well known is the origin of the park:

12
00:00:35,430 --> 00:00:40,000
unlike many public parks that started in private ownership,

13
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,550
as the garden of a large house, for instance,

14
00:00:43,550 --> 00:00:46,900
Minster was some waste land,

15
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:53,410
which people living nearby started planting with flowers in 1892.

16
00:00:53,410 --> 00:00:57,250
It was unclear who actually owned the land,

17
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:01,000
and this wasn't settled until 20 years later,

18
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,740
when the council took possession of it.

19
00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:08,500
You may have noticed the statue near one of the entrances.

20
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:10,164
It's of Diane Gosforth,

21
00:01:10,164 --> 00:01:14,740
who played a key role in the history of the park.

22
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:20,218
Once the council had become the legal owner, it planned to sell the land

23
00:01:20,218 --> 00:01:21,000
for housing.

24
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,350
Many local people wanted it to remain a place that everyone could go to,

25
00:01:26,350 --> 00:01:30,000
to enjoy the fresh air and natural environment–

26
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,480
remember the park is in a densely populated residential area.

27
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:39,798
Diane Gosforth was one of those people,

28
00:01:39,798 --> 00:01:43,500
and she organized petitions and demonstrations,

29
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:49,150
which eventually made the council change its mind about the future of the land.

30
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:55,900
Soon after this the First World War broke out, in 1914,

31
00:01:55,900 --> 00:02:00,662
and most of the park was dug up and planted with vegetables,

32
00:02:00,662 --> 00:02:02,250
which were sold locally.

33
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:07,930
At one stage the army considered taking it over for troop exercises

34
00:02:07,930 --> 00:02:13,768
and got as far as contacting the city council, then decided the park was

35
00:02:13,768 --> 00:02:16,270
too small to be of use.

36
00:02:16,270 --> 00:02:20,210
There were occasional public meetings during the war,

37
00:02:20,210 --> 00:02:24,000
in an area that had been retained as grass.

38
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,020
After the war, the park was turned back more or less to how it

39
00:02:30,020 --> 00:02:31,740
had been before 1914,

40
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:36,230
and continued almost unchanged until recently.

41
00:02:36,230 --> 00:02:44,020
Plans for transforming it were drawn up at various times, most recently in 2013,

42
00:02:44,020 --> 00:02:50,000
though they were revised in 2015, before any work had started.

43
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,505
The changes finally got going in 2016,

44
00:02:54,505 --> 00:02:59,010
and were finished on schedule last year.

45
00:02:59,010 --> 00:03:05,120
OK, let me tell you about some of the changes that have been made–

46
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:07,810
and some things that have been retained.

47
00:03:07,810 --> 00:03:12,950
If you look at this map, you'll see the familiar outline of the park,

48
00:03:12,950 --> 00:03:16,340
with the river forming the northern boundary,

49
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:20,770
and a gate in each of the other three walls.

50
00:03:20,770 --> 00:03:24,730
The statue of Diane Gosforth has been moved:

51
00:03:24,730 --> 00:03:27,830
it used to be close to the south gate,

52
00:03:27,830 --> 00:03:32,000
but it's now immediately to the north of the lily pond,

53
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,150
almost in the centre of the park,

54
00:03:34,150 --> 00:03:37,240
which makes it much more visible.

55
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:40,202
There's a new area of wooden sculptures,

56
00:03:40,202 --> 00:03:42,740
which are on the river bank,

57
00:03:42,740 --> 00:03:47,090
where the path from the east gate makes a sharp bend.

58
00:03:47,090 --> 00:03:52,000
There are two areas that are particularly intended for children.

59
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,450
The playground has been enlarged and improved,

60
00:03:55,450 --> 00:04:00,744
and that's between the river and the path that leads from the pond to

61
00:04:00,744 --> 00:04:01,500
the river.

62
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:09,500
Then there's a new maze, a circular series of paths, separated by low hedges.

63
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:12,240
That's near the west gate–

64
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:18,083
you go north from there towards the river and then turn left to reach

65
00:04:18,083 --> 00:04:18,500
it.

66
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:22,650
There have been tennis courts in the park for many years,

67
00:04:22,650 --> 00:04:26,920
and they've been doubled, from four to eight.

68
00:04:26,920 --> 00:04:30,340
They're still in the south-west corner of the park,

69
00:04:30,340 --> 00:04:33,700
where there's a right-angle bend in the path.

70
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:38,530
Something else I'd like to mention is the new fitness area.

71
00:04:38,530 --> 00:04:43,430
This is right next to the lily pond on the same side as the

72
00:04:43,430 --> 00:04:44,130
west gate.

73
00:04:44,130 --> 00:04:47,700
Now, as you're all gardeners,

74
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:51,462
I'm sure you'll like to hear about the plants that have been chosen for

75
00:04:51,462 --> 00:04:52,000
the park.
